      <a class="eyebrow" href="/guides/" data-knot style="text-decoration:none"><i class="knot"></i>Guides</a>
      <h1>Zoho or Salesforce: which CRM suits an Indian manufacturer?</h1>
      <p class="article-meta">By Sutrayaan · Updated {{ page.updated_label }} · 7-minute read</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="tight">
    <div class="wrap">
      <article class="prose">
        <p class="answer"><b>Short answer:</b> for most Indian manufacturers with fewer than about 50 people using the CRM, <b>Zoho</b> is the better fit. It costs ₹400 to ₹2,600 per user a month, is billed in rupees, and can be live in weeks.<sup><a href="#s2">2</a>,<a href="#s3">3</a></sup> <b>Salesforce</b> suits large sales teams, complex multi-country selling, or cases where a customer or parent company requires it. Its plans run from $25 to $175 and more per user a month, and it usually needs a certified partner to set up.<sup><a href="#s1">1</a>,<a href="#s9">9</a></sup></p>

        <p class="small"><b>Our interest, stated plainly:</b> Sutrayaan sets up Zoho (Bigin and Zoho CRM), not Salesforce, and we may earn a referral fee from Zoho. We have tried to be fair, used published prices, and said clearly where Salesforce is the better choice.</p>

        <h2>The difference at a glance</h2>
        <div class="table-wrap"><table>
          <thead><tr><th></th><th>Zoho (Bigin and Zoho CRM)</th><th>Salesforce (Sales Cloud)</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><b>Price per user a month</b></td><td>Bigin ₹400–1,080. Zoho CRM ₹800–2,600. Yearly billing, before GST.<sup><a href="#s2">2</a>,<a href="#s3">3</a></sup></td><td>Starter $25, Pro $100, Enterprise about $165–175. Billed yearly.<sup><a href="#s1">1</a>,<a href="#s4">4</a>,<a href="#s9">9</a></sup></td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Currency</b></td><td>Rupees, with GST invoice</td><td>Listed in US dollars, even on the India pricing page<sup><a href="#s1">1</a></sup></td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Setup cost in India</b></td><td>About ₹20,000 for a small Bigin setup, to ₹85,000–3.5 lakh for Zoho CRM through a partner<sup><a href="#s6">6</a></sup></td><td>One Indian guide puts it at ₹5 lakh to ₹5 crore and more, depending on size<sup><a href="#s5">5</a></sup></td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Time to go live</b></td><td>1 week (Bigin) to 4–8 weeks (Zoho CRM)</td><td>Usually a few months for a proper rollout</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Who runs it after go-live</b></td><td>The owner or sales head can manage it</td><td>Usually a trained admin or a partner on retainer</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Manufacturing features</b></td><td>General CRM: pipelines, quotes, price lists, approvals. Zoho also sells accounts and inventory apps in the same family.</td><td>A separate Manufacturing Cloud with sales agreements and account-based forecasting,<sup><a href="#s7">7</a></sup> at a higher price</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>How far it can be customised</b></td><td>Enough for most SMEs</td><td>Very deep, with the largest partner and app ecosystem</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>

        <h2>What 10 users cost in a year</h2>
        <p>Licence only, on yearly billing, before GST. Dollar prices are converted at about ₹85, so treat the Salesforce figures as approximate.</p>
        <div class="table-wrap"><table>
          <thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Per user a month</th><th>10 users, one year</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Zoho Bigin Express</td><td>₹400</td><td>₹48,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Zoho CRM Professional</td><td>₹1,400</td><td>₹1,68,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Zoho CRM Enterprise</td><td>₹2,400</td><td>₹2,88,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Salesforce Starter Suite</td><td>$25</td><td>About ₹2.5 lakh</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Salesforce Pro Suite</td><td>$100</td><td>About ₹10 lakh</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Salesforce Enterprise</td><td>About $165–175</td><td>About ₹17–18 lakh</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p>The gap is widest in the middle. Zoho CRM Professional has quotes, price lists and process rules for ₹1,400. The closest Salesforce plan, Pro Suite, is $100. One Indian Salesforce partner says most Indian SMEs on Salesforce end up on Enterprise, because that is where deeper customisation starts.<sup><a href="#s4">4</a></sup></p>

        <h2>When Salesforce is the right choice</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><b>A large sales team.</b> Around 100 or more CRM users, across regions or countries.</li>
          <li><b>A customer or parent company requires it.</b> Some global groups and OEM customers run on Salesforce and expect suppliers or subsidiaries to match.</li>
          <li><b>Long-term supply contracts.</b> If most revenue comes from yearly volume agreements, Manufacturing Cloud tracks planned against actual volumes and builds forecasts from them.<sup><a href="#s7">7</a></sup> It costs more than Sales Cloud. One source lists it at $275 per user a month.<sup><a href="#s8">8</a></sup></li>
          <li><b>You have an IT team.</b> Salesforce rewards companies that can employ an admin and keep improving it.</li>
          <li><b>Complex, unusual processes.</b> If you need heavy custom logic and many system integrations, Salesforce has the deeper platform.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>When Zoho is the right choice</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Fewer than about 50 CRM users.</b> This covers most Indian manufacturing SMEs.</li>
          <li><b>You want it live this quarter.</b> A small team can be working in Bigin within a week.</li>
          <li><b>Budget matters.</b> The yearly Salesforce licence for 10 users can pay for Zoho for several years.</li>
          <li><b>Nobody on staff will be a full-time CRM admin.</b></li>
          <li><b>You want rupee billing and one family of apps.</b> Zoho's CRM, accounts and inventory apps come from the same company.</li>
          <li><b>Your team works on phones.</b> Both have mobile apps. Bigin is simple enough for a sales team that has never used a CRM.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Which Zoho product: Bigin or Zoho CRM?</h2>
        <div class="table-wrap"><table>
          <thead><tr><th>Your team</th><th>Start with</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>1 to about 8 people handling enquiries, simple quotes</td><td><b>Bigin</b>, from ₹400 per user a month</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Export and dealer pipelines, quotes from price lists, approvals, owner dashboards</td><td><b>Zoho CRM</b> Professional or Enterprise</td></tr>
            <tr><td>100 or more users, global group, contract-based forecasting</td><td>Look at <b>Salesforce</b> seriously</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p>You are not locked in at the bottom. Zoho supports moving from Bigin up to Zoho CRM later.</p>

        <h2>Five questions to ask before you choose either</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><b>How many people will log in every day?</b> Pay for those, not for everyone.</li>
          <li><b>What is the full first-year cost?</b> Add licence, setup, training and GST. Ask for it in writing.</li>
          <li><b>Who will look after it after go-live?</b> If the answer is "nobody", pick the simpler tool.</li>
          <li><b>Is our process clear?</b> Software won't fix an unclear process. Agree the steps from enquiry to order first.</li>
          <li><b>Can we get our data out?</b> Check that you can export customers, deals and quotes at any time.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2>What we do</h2>
        <p>Sutrayaan sets up Zoho Bigin for small teams from ₹20,000, and Zoho CRM for growing manufacturers from ₹75,000, starting with a short process review. If the review shows you need Salesforce, we will say so. Details are on our <a href="/crm-for-manufacturers/">CRM page</a>, and you can read a <a href="/crm-for-manufacturers/process-review-sample/">sample process review</a>.</p>

        <div class="faq" style="margin-top:12px">
          <details><summary>Is Zoho good enough for a manufacturing company?</summary><p>For most small and mid-sized manufacturers, yes. It handles enquiries, quotes, price lists, approvals, dealer and export pipelines, and owner reports. Very large teams or contract-heavy businesses may need Salesforce.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Why is Salesforce so much more expensive?</summary><p>It is built for large companies. The licence is priced in dollars, the plans most firms need start at $100 per user a month, and setup usually needs a certified partner.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Can we start on Zoho and move to Salesforce later?</summary><p>Yes. Both let you export your customers and deals. A clear process and clean data matter more than the first tool you pick.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Do both work with IndiaMART and WhatsApp?</summary><p>Both can receive IndiaMART leads and connect to WhatsApp, through built-in features or connector tools. Check what your plan includes before you buy.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Is there a free version of either?</summary><p>Zoho CRM is free for up to three users, and Bigin is free for one. Salesforce also lists a free tier with limited features. Free plans suit a trial, not a sales team.</p></details>
        </div>

